// Per-thread module state — `@threadlocal let` (§11.11, #1411). The storage
// form `sched.zig`'s `threadlocal var tls: ?*Worker` needs, and therefore the
// blocker #1364 (scheduler) sat behind.
//
// LINUX ONLY, and that is the feature's shape rather than a test limitation:
// ELF reaches a per-thread cell by adding a link-time-known offset to the
// thread pointer (`mrs TPIDR_EL0` on aarch64, `mov fs:[0]` on x86-64), which is
// pure arithmetic. Mach-O instead loads a TLV descriptor and CALLS its resolver
// thunk, so emission there is refused rather than silently placed in `__data` —
// which would be one process-wide cell wearing a per-thread name. See §11.11.
//
// WHAT THIS TEST DOES AND DOES NOT PROVE. The shipped scheduler is pinned to a
// single worker (`root.zig`: `Scheduler.init(1)`), so every green thread in
// this process runs on ONE OS thread. This test therefore proves the per-thread
// cell is correctly LAID OUT, INITIALIZED and ADDRESSED — not that two threads
// see two cells. Distinctness across OS threads was verified separately, by
// temporarily lifting that pin: four workers gave four distinct cell addresses
// from this same declaration, and removing `@threadlocal` collapsed it to one.
// That evidence cannot live here as a regression guard until the pin lifts,
// which is #1364's own work.
//
// Four properties, all deterministic:
//
//   (a) The initializer image reaches the thread. `counter` starts at 7 — the
//       template's bytes, copied into this thread's TLS block by the loader.
//       A cell zeroed instead of initialized fails here.
//
//   (b) Persistence across calls, exactly as process-wide state has it.
//
//   (c) `@nosplit` reachability. `bump` is `@nosplit`, so the checker has
//       proved its body neither allocates nor reaches a safepoint, and it
//       still reads and writes per-thread state. This is a COMPILE-time
//       property: the ELF access sequence is call-free, so it is legal here.
//       An implementation that reached the cell through a call could not
//       compile this function at all.
//
//   (d) `ptrOf` yields the cell's address, so per-thread state is addressable
//       for the atomic builtins the same way process-wide state is.

@threadlocal let counter: i64 = 7
@threadlocal let other: i64 = 42

@nosplit function bump(): i64 {
  counter = counter + 1
  return counter
}

function main() {
  // (a) + (b): 7 -> 8, 9, 10.
  let a = bump()
  let b = bump()
  let c = bump()

  // (d): the address is stable across reads, and writing through it is
  // writing the cell.
  let p = ptrOf(counter)
  let same = 0
  if (int(p) == int(ptrOf(counter))) { same = 1 }

  // A second declaration gets its own cell rather than aliasing the first.
  let disjoint = 0
  if (int(ptrOf(other)) != int(ptrOf(counter))) { disjoint = 1 }

  print("${a} ${b} ${c} ${same} ${disjoint} ${other}\n")
}
